// Bit-for-bit parity test between NocturNation's C++ port of the PixMob IR
// encoder (include/pixmob_protocol.h) and the upstream Python reference
// (github.com/jamesw343/PixMob_IR).
//
// The first five reference vectors (RED_PUNCHY, BLUE_GROUP22_C16,
// TWO_COLORS_RED_THEN_GREEN, SET_GROUP_ID, SET_GROUP_SEL) were generated by
// running the Python encoder at jamesw343/PixMob_IR commit HEAD (cloned
// 2026-05-06) for the same inputs the C++ test calls below.
//
// The remaining vectors (TwoColors / CycleProfiles / SetColor variants
// landed in Epic 11 B1 on 2026-06-17) come from tools/pixmob_parity_gen.py,
// a faithful Python port of the encoder. Its faithfulness is verified
// inside this test by REF_TWO_COLORS_RED_GREEN_DUP, which exercises the
// Python port against the same inputs as the original jamesw343-derived
// REF_TWO_COLORS_RED_THEN_GREEN; the two MUST match byte-for-byte for any
// of the Epic 11 vectors to be trustworthy.
//
// To regenerate after upstream changes:
//   1. Update tools/pixmob_parity_gen.py with whatever the change is.
//   2. Run `python3 tools/pixmob_parity_gen.py` and paste the output below
//      the existing arrays (or replace the existing ones if they changed).
//   3. Run `pio test -e native -f test_pixmob_parity` and confirm green.
//
// NOTE on buildCycleProfiles: its `profileMask` argument is described by
// our port as an 8-bit mask, but upstream's `CommandSetConfig` interprets
// the same bits as a profile_id_lo / profile_id_hi range (per architecture
// spec §8.2 footnote). The BYTE encoding is identical either way -
// numerical values pass through unchanged - so byte-level parity tests
// work. Fixing the API parameter name to match upstream semantics is
// captured as a B2 prerequisite in Epic 11.

#include <cstdio>
#include <unity.h>
#include "pixmob_protocol.h"

